  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers pay for technical quality and good coordination. They judge you by proof of one strength and willingness to learn the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is describing, in one sentence, which kind of problem you want to be known for solving.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and management vs technology career path: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools write code, plans and reports. Routine versions of both paths shrink.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judgment, trust and learning speed hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to draft and check; later, own decisions and teams.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general management vs technology career path path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Technology first, management later</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Credible base</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Slower title growth</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Management first, skills later</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Faster people exposure</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs technical learning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Bridge roles</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Keeps both open</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs clear proof</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn management vs technology career path cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Management vs technology career path is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM lessons.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube lessons and public documentation.</li>
    <li>Free practice datasets and open software.</li>
    <li>A senior or teacher who reviews your sample.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In twelve weeks, build one technical sample, run one small project and write a decision note.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

